<h2 id="kdp-drm-opt-in">Amazon Quietly Changes KDP DRM from Opt-Out to Opt-In</h2>

<p>In a move that signals the biggest shift in Amazon's ebook DRM policy since the launch of Kindle Direct Publishing, the company has changed its copyright protection settings for self-published titles from <strong>opt-out</strong> to <strong>opt-in</strong>. As of early July 2026, new KDP submissions are no longer DRM-protected by default — authors must actively check a box to enable Amazon's digital rights management on their ebooks.</p>

<p>The change was confirmed across multiple outlets including TechCrunch, Moneycontrol, and Pocket-lint, each independently reporting that Amazon had updated the KDP publishing dashboard. Previously, the "Enable Digital Rights Management (DRM)" checkbox was pre-checked during title setup. Authors who wanted DRM-free distribution had to know to uncheck it — a step many missed. Now the checkbox is unchecked by default, and authors who want DRM protection must deliberately enable it.</p>

<p>This shift follows a chain of related policy changes over the past year: Amazon's December 2025 decision to allow EPUB downloads for DRM-free KDP titles, the gradual rollout of a "DRM-Free" indicator on Kindle product pages (first spotted in June 2026), and the July 2026 royalty rate increase for self-publishers. Taken together, these changes paint a picture of Amazon slowly loosening its grip on the ebook format ecosystem — and handing more control to authors.</p>

<h2 id="what-actually-changed">What Actually Changed in the KDP Dashboard</h2>

<p>The KDP publishing flow now looks different for new title submissions. Here is what authors will see:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Setting</th>
      <th>Before July 2026</th>
      <th>After July 2026</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
